## Solution

We evaluate
$$L = \lim_{n\to\infty} n\int_1^e x^a (\log x)^n\,dx.$$

**Substitution.** Let $u = \log x$, so $x = e^u$, $dx = e^u\,du$. The bounds become $u=0$ (at $x=1$) and $u=1$ (at $x=e$). Then
$$\int_1^e x^a(\log x)^n\,dx = \int_0^1 e^{au}\cdot u^n \cdot e^u\,du = \int_0^1 u^n\, e^{(a+1)u}\,du.$$

Hence
$$L = \lim_{n\to\infty} n\int_0^1 u^n\, e^{(a+1)u}\,du.$$

**Reduction to a standard form.** Set $f(u) = e^{(a+1)u}$, which is continuous on $[0,1]$. We claim that for any continuous $f$ on $[0,1]$,
$$\lim_{n\to\infty} n\int_0^1 u^n f(u)\,du = f(1).$$

**Proof of the claim.** Substitute $t = n(1-u)$, i.e. $u = 1 - t/n$, $du = -dt/n$. The bounds $u\in[0,1]$ become $t\in[n,0]$, so
$$n\int_0^1 u^n f(u)\,du = \int_0^n \left(1-\frac{t}{n}\right)^n f\!\left(1-\frac{t}{n}\right)dt.$$

For each fixed $t\ge 0$, as $n\to\infty$:
- $\left(1-\frac{t}{n}\right)^n \to e^{-t}$,
- $f\!\left(1-\frac{t}{n}\right) \to f(1)$.

To justify passing the limit inside the integral, note that $0\le \left(1-\frac{t}{n}\right)^n \le e^{-t}$ for $0\le t\le n$ (using $\log(1-s)\le -s$), and $f$ is bounded on $[0,1]$, say $|f|\le M$. Thus the integrand is dominated by $M e^{-t}$, which is integrable on $[0,\infty)$. By the dominated convergence theorem,
$$\lim_{n\to\infty}\int_0^n \left(1-\frac{t}{n}\right)^n f\!\left(1-\frac{t}{n}\right)dt = \int_0^\infty e^{-t} f(1)\,dt = f(1)\int_0^\infty e^{-t}\,dt = f(1).$$

This proves the claim.

**Applying the claim.** With $f(u) = e^{(a+1)u}$, we get $f(1) = e^{a+1}$. Therefore
$$L = e^{a+1}.$$

This is valid for every real $a$, since $e^{(a+1)u}$ is continuous (indeed smooth) on $[0,1]$.

$$\boxed{e^{a+1}}$$

